Enhancement: lock-free operations and two-phase prune

Vaultic gains the final rustic-parity concurrency and prune features:

Lock-free operations (opt-in via `VAULTIC_FEATURES=lock-free=true`): read-only
and append-only commands (`backup`, `restore`, `snapshots`, `ls`, `copy`, ...)
no longer create lock files but remain writable. Because index writes are
additive, concurrent lock-free clients cannot corrupt the repository, and stale
lock files no longer block routine backups. Commands that modify existing data
(`prune`, `forget`, `repair`, `key`, `config`, `tag`, `rewrite`, ...) always
take an exclusive lock regardless. Lock-free is opt-in because it reorders
backend list operations, which requires coordinated clients on
eventually-consistent storage.

Two-phase prune (via `VAULTIC_FEATURES=two-phase-prune=true`): `prune
--keep-delete` performs only the repack and index-writing phase and defers
deletion of superseded pack/index files to a later run, shortening the
exclusive window and letting the repack phase overlap with concurrent backups.
`--instant-delete` (the default) keeps the single-phase behavior. Prune never
deletes files that were not part of its own plan, so concurrent backups are
unaffected.

Prune extras: `--max-repack` accepts an absolute size, a percentage of the
repository size (e.g. `10%`), or `unlimited` (superset of `--max-repack-size`);
`--repack-all` repacks every pack (to change pack size or compression);
`--fast-repack` documents index-trusted repack planning; and
`--early-delete-index` removes superseded index files before the pack files are
deleted, freeing index space earlier.

